<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Search for a meaning, inspect its hierarchy and clinical use, then save a concept set.
</div>

**You need:** the client packages and the Opal login from
[Getting started](book-00-start.html). This chapter starts with `omop.gibleed`;
later sections switch to the questionnaire resources and `omop.synthea`.

## Start this chapter

```{r book-local-server, include = FALSE}
# Outputs come from a local DataSHIELD server holding the same resources as the Opal in the text.
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/connection.R")
```

```{r chapter-setup, eval = FALSE}
library(dsOMOPClient)
conns <- ds.omop.login("https://localhost:8443", user = "administrator",
  password = "password", resource = "omop.gibleed", server = "site")$conns
```

```{r chapter-setup-local-2, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_connect("gibleed")$conns
```

## Search a name, then narrow the meaning

A concept is a public vocabulary meaning with a stable numeric ID. Its **domain**
is the kind of fact, such as Condition or Measurement; its **vocabulary** is the
coding system. Standard concepts (`S`) are preferred OMOP targets. Classification
concepts (`C`) group meanings; non-standard source codes may map to standard ones.

```{r search}
hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", conns = conns)$pooled
hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "domain_id", "vocabulary_id")]
```

The word matches Asthma, Childhood asthma and Asthma screening, a procedure. Keep the ID beside the name:
a familiar word alone does not specify which meaning you intend.

```{r filtered-search}
conditions <- ds.omop.concept.search("Asthma", domain = "Condition",
  vocabulary = "SNOMED", standard_only = TRUE, valid = TRUE, conns = conns)$pooled
conditions[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept", "concept_class_id")]
asthma_id <- conditions$concept_id[conditions$concept_name == "Asthma"]
```

Asthma is **317009** and Childhood asthma is **4051466**. Both are standard
SNOMED clinical findings in the Condition domain. The filters state the intended
kind of code; they have not yet selected any people.

## Read a concept and its mappings

Look up the chosen ID to confirm its identity:

```{r inspect}
asthma_info <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(asthma_id, conns = conns)$pooled
asthma_info
```

The lookup confirms that 317009 means Asthma. For vocabulary validity dates,
use the richer search metadata:

```{r validity, table.row.names = FALSE}
conditions[conditions$concept_id == asthma_id,
  c("concept_id", "concept_name", "valid_start_date", "valid_end_date", "invalid_reason")]
```

The validity interval is about the vocabulary entry, not patients' diagnosis
dates. A missing `invalid_reason` means the entry has not been marked invalid.

A **Maps to** relationship connects a source code to its standard target. Find
gastrointestinal haemorrhage to see a real mapping in this vocabulary:

```{r bleeding-search}
bleeding <- ds.omop.concept.search("Gastrointestinal hemorrhage", domain = "Condition",
  conns = conns)$pooled
bleeding[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "vocabulary_id", "standard_concept")]
bleeding_id <- bleeding$concept_id[bleeding$concept_name == "Gastrointestinal hemorrhage"]
```

The standard SNOMED concept is **192671**. Inspect the mapping edges touching it:

```{r maps-to}
mappings <- ds.omop.concept.relationships(bleeding_id,
  relationship_id = "Maps to", conns = conns)$pooled
mappings[c("concept_id", "related_concept_id", "related_concept_name",
  "relationship_id", "direction")]
```

`reverse` means the related concept **35208414** is the source of the mapping
into the requested target **192671**. Inspect that source code explicitly:

```{r mapping-source}
source_code <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, concept_id = 35208414L,
  standard_only = FALSE, conns = conns)$pooled
source_code[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "vocabulary_id",
  "concept_code", "standard_concept")]
```

The source is ICD10CM **K92.2**, Gastrointestinal hemorrhage, unspecified; its
standard status is missing. It maps to the standard SNOMED target. A mapping
translates a code; adding descendants instead broadens a clinical definition.

## Walk down and up the hierarchy

Descendants are more specific meanings; ancestors are broader meanings. Inspect
them before including a whole branch in your study.

```{r descendants}
desc <- ds.omop.concept.descendants(asthma_id, include_self = FALSE, conns = conns)$pooled
desc[c("concept_id", "concept_name")]
child_id <- desc$concept_id[desc$concept_name == "Childhood asthma"]
```

Childhood asthma (4051466) is a descendant of Asthma (317009). A set that includes
descendants will include both meanings unless you exclude one.

```{r ancestors}
parents <- ds.omop.concept.ancestors(child_id, conns = conns)$pooled
parents[parents$direction == "ancestor",
  c("concept_id", "concept_name", "direction")]
```

The `ancestor` row leads back to Asthma. This API can return both directions;
selecting `direction == "ancestor"` is what makes this an upward walk.

## Check that the concept occurs here

A catalogue entry does not establish clinical use. Despite its name,
`concept.prevalence()` returns protected **person counts** by default. A
prevalence estimate would also need a defined population, period and denominator.

```{r occurrence-counts, table.row.names = FALSE}
use <- ds.omop.concept.prevalence("condition_occurrence", metric = "persons",
  top_n = 20, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
use[use$concept_id %in% c(asthma_id, child_id, bleeding_id),
  c("concept_id", "concept_name", "n_persons")]
```

Only gastrointestinal haemorrhage appears in these top 20 results, with a band
of 475–479 people. Neither asthma concept made this top list. Their absence is
not evidence of zero use; the targeted locator below answers that question.

Locate both IDs to check which tables and columns actually use them:

```{r locate, table.row.names = FALSE}
locations <- ds.omop.concept.locate(c(asthma_id, child_id), scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
locations$concept_name <- conditions$concept_name[match(locations$concept_id, conditions$concept_id)]
locations[locations$table_name == "condition_occurrence",
  c("concept_id", "concept_name", "table_name", "n_records", "n_persons")]
```

The record bands begin at 5 for Asthma and 95 for Childhood asthma: 5–9 and
95–99 records respectively. Looking only for the seed would miss most records
in this branch. Counts are rounded down to multiples of five, so do not treat
these as exact counts or calculate a precise percentage from them.

## Save the definition, including exclusions

A concept set records your selection rule, including whether descendants belong.
It is a local definition, not a list of patients.

```{r concept-set}
asthma <- omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = TRUE)
concept_file <- "book-concepts.rds"
saveRDS(list(asthma = asthma), concept_file)
asthma
```

The printed definition includes the seed and requests its descendants.
`book-concepts.rds` now stores that definition for reuse. Setting
`descendants = FALSE` would select only the seed.

**Exercise 1.** Keep Asthma's children but exclude the seed itself. Save the
set and read it back. Does the saved definition preserve the exclusion?

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r excluded-set}
children_only <- omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = TRUE,
  exclude = omop_concept_set(asthma_id, descendants = FALSE))
saved_sets <- readRDS(concept_file)
saved_sets$children_only <- children_only
saveRDS(saved_sets, concept_file)
saved_sets <- readRDS(concept_file)
saved_sets$children_only
```

The reloaded definition still requests descendants and excludes 317009 itself.
An excluded set could also include descendants if you wanted to remove an
entire branch. This file contains definitions, not patient data.

</details>

## Find inpatient and outpatient visits

Use `visit_concept_id` for the setting; `visit_type_concept_id` describes the
record's provenance. Discover the settings before using their IDs:

```{r visits}
visit_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Visit", domain = "Visit", conns = conns)$pooled
visit_catalogue <- visit_hits[visit_hits$concept_name %in%
  c("Inpatient Visit", "Outpatient Visit"), ]
visit_catalogue[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
visit_ids <- setNames(visit_catalogue$concept_id, visit_catalogue$concept_name)
visit_sets <- lapply(visit_ids, omop_concept_set)
```

Inpatient Visit is **9201** and Outpatient Visit is **9202**. Both exist in the
vocabulary. Check their clinical use separately:

```{r visit-use}
visit_use <- ds.omop.concept.locate(unname(visit_ids), scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
visit_use$concept_name <- names(visit_ids)[match(visit_use$concept_id, visit_ids)]
visit_use[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "n_records", "n_persons")]
```

Only Inpatient Visit is returned: its bands begin at 1,035 records and 890 people.
The public GiBleed data record only inpatient visits; Outpatient Visit is unused.
In an unfamiliar private dataset, an absent locator row could also be withheld.

## Find the questionnaire questions, answers and causes

Switch to the separate synthetic dataset described in
[chapter 0](book-00-start.html#the-resources-on-the-server). Its local question and
answer codes are teaching examples, not codes to copy into a clinical study.

```{r questionnaire-catalogue, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.questionnaire", conns = conns)
```

```{r questionnaire-catalogue-local-19, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_switch("questionnaire")$conns
```

```{r questionnaire-catalogue-20}
questionnaire <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, standard_only = FALSE,
  limit = 100, conns = conns)$pooled
questionnaire[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "domain_id")]
question_ids <- setNames(questionnaire$concept_id, questionnaire$concept_name)
education_set <- omop_concept_set(unname(question_ids["Education question"]))
cause_sets <- lapply(question_ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")], omop_concept_set)
```

Education question **900001** selects observations; Secondary **900101** and
University **900102** are its coded answers. Fever question **900002** uses Yes
**900301** and No **900302**. The death causes are Myocardial infarction **900201**
and Pneumonia **900202**. Visits use the Visit domain. Study medicine **950001**
supports drug-history lessons; weight **950002**, with kilogram **9529**, supports
numeric exercises. The default local question and cause codes have no external
mapping or hierarchy.

```{r questionnaire-presence}
question_presence <- ds.omop.concept.prevalence("observation", scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
question_presence[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "n_persons")]
```

The person bands for education and fever both begin at 20, meaning 20–24 people.
The public dataset has 24 respondents to each question. Later categorical tables
can release those exact counts when their cells pass the table's checks.

```{r cause-presence, table.row.names = FALSE}
cause_use <- ds.omop.concept.locate(unname(question_ids[c("Myocardial infarction", "Pneumonia")]),
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
cause_use$concept_name <- names(question_ids)[match(cause_use$concept_id, question_ids)]
cause_use[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "table_name", "n_records", "n_persons")]
```

The cause bands begin at 20 for infarction and 10 for pneumonia: 20–24 and 10–14
people. They contain the dataset's exact totals of 24 and 12. The different
representations are not conflicting counts.

**Exercise 2.** ATC classes are classification concepts. Can a standard-only
search miss them even when they are present? Use the optional teaching catalogue.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r atc-default, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.questionnaire_vocab", conns = conns)
```

```{r atc-default-local-24, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_switch("questionnaire", categories = TRUE)$conns
```

```{r atc-default-25}
atc_default <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", conns = conns)$pooled
atc_default
```

`NULL` means the search returned no matching concepts: the default asks for
standard concepts (`S`).
Request classification concepts (`C`) instead:

```{r atc-classification}
atc <- ds.omop.concept.search(NULL, vocabulary = "ATC", standard = "C", conns = conns)$pooled
atc[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
```

Two classes appear: **960001** and **960002**. These are explicitly invented
teaching classes, used in the drug-utilisation lesson. The lesson here is the
search filter: a missing standard-only hit does not prove a class is absent.

</details>

## Find haemoglobin, glucose and their units in Synthea

Switch to the numeric resource. The vocabulary spells haemoglobin “Hemoglobin”.
Select a precise test and specimen, rather than every name containing the word.

```{r numeric-catalogue, eval = FALSE}
ds.omop.disconnect(conns = conns)
ds.omop.connect(resource = "omop.synthea", conns = conns)
```

```{r numeric-catalogue-local-28, include = FALSE}
conns <- book_switch("numeric")$conns
```

```{r numeric-catalogue-29}
lab_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Hemoglobin", domain = "Measurement", conns = conns)$pooled
lab_hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
hb_id <- lab_hits$concept_id[lab_hits$concept_name == "Hemoglobin [Mass/volume] in Blood"]
```

Choose **3000963**, Hemoglobin [Mass/volume] in Blood. A panel and a haemoglobin
fraction are different measurements even though their names share the word.

```{r glucose-catalogue}
glucose_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Glucose", domain = "Measurement", conns = conns)$pooled
glucose_hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
glucose_id <- glucose_hits$concept_id[glucose_hits$concept_name == "Glucose [Mass/volume] in Blood"]
lab_sets <- list(haemoglobin = omop_concept_set(hb_id), glucose = omop_concept_set(glucose_id))
```

Blood glucose is **3000483**. Serum or Plasma glucose and urine glucose are
separate concepts; do not combine all search hits without deciding which
specimens and concentration measures answer your question.

```{r lab-units}
units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id", concept_id = hb_id,
  scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
names(units)[names(units) == "value"] <- "unit_concept_id"
units[c("unit_concept_id", "concept_name", "n")]
```

Haemoglobin uses **8713**, gram per deciliter (g/dL), for 105–109 recorded values.
The count describes recorded unit use; a unit's mere presence in the vocabulary would not establish this.

### When a unit count is withheld

For Serum or Plasma glucose, the requested unit count lacks enough support.
Keep the server's explanation instead of treating an unavailable result as zero:

<!-- expected-refusal:start -->
```{r glucose-unit-refusal}
serum_glucose_id <- glucose_hits$concept_id[glucose_hits$concept_name ==
  "Glucose [Mass/volume] in Serum or Plasma"]
serum_glucose_units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id",
  concept_id = serum_glucose_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)
serum_glucose_units$meta$warnings
```
<!-- expected-refusal:end -->

The aggregate is unavailable; this does not establish that the concept has no
records. Use the separately selected Blood glucose for the next exercise.

**Exercise 3.** Check Blood glucose's unit and add the lab and questionnaire
sets to `book-concepts.rds` without discarding the saved asthma exclusions.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>

```{r glucose-units}
glucose_units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id",
  concept_id = glucose_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
names(glucose_units)[names(glucose_units) == "value"] <- "unit_concept_id"
glucose_units[c("unit_concept_id", "concept_name", "n")]
saved_sets <- readRDS(concept_file)
saved_sets$sets <- lab_sets
saved_sets$visit_sets <- visit_sets
saved_sets$education <- education_set
saved_sets$causes <- cause_sets
saved_sets$questionnaire_catalogue <- questionnaire
saved_sets$glucose_catalogue <- glucose_hits
saveRDS(saved_sets, concept_file)
```

Blood glucose uses **8840**, milligram per deciliter (mg/dL), for 225–229 records.
Reading the file before adding entries preserves `asthma` and `children_only`. To reuse education
in your own later session, read `readRDS("book-concepts.rds")$education`.
The following chapters name the IDs from this catalogue so they can also run
independently in a fresh session.

</details>

## Find measured BMI and its recorded unit

Measured BMI is a recorded value, distinct from calculating it from height and weight.

```{r bmi-discovery}
bmi_hits <- ds.omop.concept.search("Body mass index", domain = "Measurement",
  vocabulary = "LOINC", conns = conns)$pooled
bmi_hits[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "standard_concept")]
bmi_id <- bmi_hits$concept_id[bmi_hits$concept_name == "Body mass index (BMI) [Ratio]"]
```

Choose the standard LOINC measurement **3038553**, Body mass index (BMI) [Ratio].
The repeated-measures chapter uses this definition.

```{r bmi-use}
bmi_use <- ds.omop.concept.locate(bmi_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
bmi_use$concept_name <- bmi_hits$concept_name[match(bmi_use$concept_id, bmi_hits$concept_id)]
bmi_use[c("concept_id", "concept_name", "n_records", "n_persons")]
```

The bands begin at 435 records and 25 people, showing repeated measurements.
Check the recorded unit before summarising those values:

```{r bmi-unit}
bmi_units <- ds.omop.value.counts("measurement", "unit_concept_id",
  concept_id = bmi_id, scope = "pooled", conns = conns)$pooled
names(bmi_units)[names(bmi_units) == "value"] <- "unit_concept_id"
bmi_units[c("unit_concept_id", "concept_name", "n")]
saved_sets <- readRDS(concept_file)
saved_sets$measured_bmi <- omop_concept_set(bmi_id)
saved_sets$bmi_units <- bmi_units
saveRDS(saved_sets, concept_file)
```

BMI uses **9531**, kilogram per square meter. Its record band also begins at 435.
The saved set and unit record preserve both parts of the measurement definition.

## Recap

Search, inspect meanings and mappings, check the hierarchy and clinical use,
then save the definition. Keep IDs alongside names during discovery; analysis
tables can then present the selected concepts as named categories.

**Next:** [5. A first recipe](book-05-recipe.html).
